To fix race conditions in parallel automated response workflows, you need to synchronize access to shared resources using locks or other synchronization primitives. You can use techniques such as `try-lock` or `try-lock-exchange` to ensure that only one thread can access the shared resource at a time. You can also use atomic operations to update the shared resource in a single, uninterruptible step. Additionally, you can use a semaphore or a flag to indicate the status of the workflow and avoid deadlocks. By implementing these synchronization techniques, you can ensure that parallel automated response workflows are executed concurrently without encountering race conditions. 

